PVP COMPANION MODULE — README-ANCHORED VERIFICATION
Repo: github.com/ArcFlood/companion-module-renewedvision-pvp @ main
Date: 2026-06-19
Method: engineering:debug, anchored on the README's stated feature claims
(the repo has no issues, so the README plays the role issue #1 played for
arcOS). Each claim is verified against source.

CLAIM SET (from README)
"This fork adds expanded live variables and grouped feedbacks for playlists,
cues, layers, transport state, effects, transitions, target sets, blend modes,
presets, and workspace status while keeping the original PVP control actions
intact."
Plus three example variables:
  $(pvp:playlist_0_cue_0_name)
  $(pvp:transport_0_time_remaining_clock)
  $(pvp:layer_0_opacity)


================================================================
CLAIM VERIFICATION
================================================================

C-A — "expanded live variables ... for [10 categories]".  STATUS: TRUE
  Evidence: src/variables-core.js builds definitions+values for every claimed
  category — playlists and per-cue vars, per-layer vars, per-transport vars,
  workspace effects/transition/effectPreset, plus catalog vars for targetSets,
  blendModes, layerPresets, effectPresets, transitions, availableEffects.
  buildVariableDefinitions + buildVariableValues cover all ten.

C-B — "grouped feedbacks" for the same categories.  STATUS: TRUE
  Evidence: src/feedbacks-core.js (764 lines) defines feedback option groups for
  playlist, cue, active layer, target set, effect preset, layer preset, blend
  mode, blend, transition, effects, plus workspace-level catalog feedbacks
  (playlist_count, cue_count_total, layer_count, active_layer_count,
  active_cue_count). feedbacks.js wires them via a live state getter
  (getPvpVariablesState), so feedbacks read the polled state. Claim holds.

C-C — three example variables resolve.  STATUS: TRUE
  Evidence: playlist_{i}_cue_{j}_name, transport_{i}_time_remaining_clock, and
  layer_{i}_opacity (via the _opacity suffix in addLayerValues) are all emitted
  by variables-core.js. The README examples are real.

C-D — "keeping the original PVP control actions intact".  STATUS: MOSTLY TRUE — 1 EXCEPTION
  Evidence: 29 actions are defined in src/actions.js; the standard control set
  (clear/mute/unmute/select/hide/unhide layer, select/trigger playlist, trigger
  cue, trigger playlist/cue, trigger layer/playlist/cue, target set, layer
  preset, effect preset, opacity absolute, track matte, etc.) is present and
  uses the original doCommand path.
  EXCEPTION: the RELATIVE opacity branch of setOpacity is broken — it calls
  self.getRest()/self.postRest(), which are not defined in this fork, and uses
  an undefined variable opt. See V1 below. Absolute opacity works; relative does
  not. So "intact" is true except for this one regression.

C-E — backup-instance fan-out (HELP.md claim).  STATUS: TRUE
  Evidence: setPvpIps builds arrTargets with primary + backup when both validate
  (isPortValid). doCommand loops arrTargets and posts to each. The "one action
  hits both PVPs" behavior described in HELP.md is implemented.


================================================================
DEFECTS SURFACED WHILE VERIFYING
================================================================

V1 — Relative-opacity branch is broken (undefined methods + var). SEVERITY: HIGH
  File: src/actions.js setOpacity (~305-328). self.getRest/self.postRest are
  never defined; opt.idx/opt.opacity should be idx/opacity. Throws at runtime.
  This is the one place the "actions intact" claim fails.
  Fix: rewrite relative mode to read current opacity from polled state
  (pvpVariablesState layer.opacity) and doCommand the new absolute value;
  replace opt.* with idx/opacity.

V2 — No HTTP timeouts on either command or poll path.  SEVERITY: MEDIUM
  api.js (node-rest-client) and variables-api.js (fetch) both lack request
  timeouts. A stalled PVP hangs commands and can freeze the poll loop via the
  in-flight guard.
  Fix: AbortSignal.timeout on fetch; requestConfig timeout on node-rest-client.

V3 — Manifest metadata is placeholder.  SEVERITY: LOW (release-blocking for distribution)
  File: companion/manifest.json — version "0.0.0", runtime apiVersion "0.0.0".
  package.json says version "2.0.0". Mismatch; "0.0.0" will not pass Bitfocus
  module submission and gives users no version signal.
  Fix: set a real semver in both, align apiVersion to the @companion-module/base
  version actually targeted (~1.4.1).


================================================================
NOTE
================================================================
The README is accurate about WHAT exists — the expanded variables and grouped
feedbacks are all really there and wired to live polled state. The gap is not
overstated features; it is one broken action branch (V1) and HTTP-robustness
plus release-metadata polish (V2, V3). Deeper code-quality findings (TLS,
encoding, fan-out, dual HTTP stacks) are in the independent scan, not visible
from README claims alone.
